{
  "duration": 31.686,
  "lead": 0.35,
  "outro": 1.0,
  "lines": [
    {
      "i": 0,
      "text": "What actually happens if you pinch your nose and hold in a sneeze?",
      "start": 0.35,
      "end": 3.322
    },
    {
      "i": 1,
      "text": "A normal sneeze blasts air out of your lungs at over one hundred miles per hour.",
      "start": 3.602,
      "end": 7.512
    },
    {
      "i": 2,
      "text": "When you clamp your mouth and nose shut, that massive pressure has nowhere to escape.",
      "start": 7.792,
      "end": 12.028
    },
    {
      "i": 3,
      "text": "The explosive force gets redirected backwards up your Eustachian tubes.",
      "start": 12.308,
      "end": 16.054
    },
    {
      "i": 4,
      "text": "This can violently rupture your delicate eardrum, causing permanent hearing damage.",
      "start": 16.334,
      "end": 20.399
    },
    {
      "i": 5,
      "text": "In extreme cases, the pressure can tear your pharynx or push air into your chest cavity.",
      "start": 20.679,
      "end": 25.272
    },
    {
      "i": 6,
      "text": "So whenever you feel a sneeze coming, never hold it in. Just let it fly!",
      "start": 25.552,
      "end": 30.386
    }
  ],
